---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Uses | Titan" description="Tools, setup, and workflow used by Titan.">
  <section class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold sm:text-4xl">Uses</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      Current tools and workflow for building and testing runtime destruction systems.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
    <article class="surface">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Primary Stack</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
        <li>Unity 2022/2023 LTS (current production target)</li>
        <li>Visual Studio + Rider (mixed workflow)</li>
        <li>GitHub + Git LFS where needed</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="surface">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Profiling and Debugging</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
        <li>Unity Profiler and frame debugger captures</li>
        <li>Runtime debug overlays for fracture state and timings</li>
        <li>Repeatable scene test harnesses for regressions</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="surface md:col-span-2">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Workflow Notes</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
        Authoring and runtime paths are kept separate, with deterministic seeds used for reproducible test runs.
        Shared data structures are kept engine-agnostic so Unreal migration work can focus on integration details
        instead of rewrites.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
